<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palakus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Palacus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palacus was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in the late 2nd century BCE, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scilurus">Scilurus</a>. Ruling from Scythian Neapolis near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simferopol">Simferopol</a>, he pressed the Greek city of Chersonesus, which called in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>. His forces, joined by the Sarmatian Roxolani, were defeated by the Pontic general Diophantus in campaigns around 110–107 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palakus.jpg" alt="Palakus" width="320" height="569" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Venzz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_of_Palacus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
